  "text": "To close, three questions about setting up a training run, answered quickly. Which loss goes with which output? A softmax output with cross-entropy for several classes, or a sigmoid with binary cross-entropy for two. For regression, a plain linear output with mean squared error, or a robust loss when outliers matter. Squared error on a sigmoid output stalls once the unit saturates. Why does initialization matter? Start every weight equal, and the units in a layer stay copies of each other. Start at the wrong scale, and the signal explodes or vanishes with depth. Through ten ReLU layers, a weight spread of one blows it up to twenty-six billion, and point oh one shrinks it to almost nothing. He initialization, scaled to each layer's width, keeps it on the same scale; Xavier is the version for tanh. And epoch, batch and iteration? An epoch is one pass over the training set. A batch is the examples behind one update, and an iteration is one update. Ten thousand examples in batches of thirty-two make three hundred and thirteen iterations an epoch. A network that does well on held-out data has still only won offline. Whether it helps real users is decided by a test. Next: designing the experiment.",
